1
00:00:01,000 --> 00:00:08,850
Slide 28 analyzes "FINANCIAL RISK: UNCONTROLLED WALLET: Why autonomous agents need strict budgetary guardrails."

2
00:00:09,750 --> 00:00:20,684
Look at the left card: "THE THREAT": An unconstrained agent executing recursive API loops or falling victim to a malicious prompt injection could drain thousands of dollars in cloud API credits in minutes.

3
00:00:20,684 --> 00:00:31,780
I personally consulted for an early-stage startup where a rogue multi-agent loop ran overnight without rate limits, racking up a twelve-thousand-dollar bill on an unmonitored OpenAI API key in just six hours!

4
00:00:32,680 --> 00:00:44,560
Look at the right card: "THE SOLUTION": Hard spending limits, pre-funded virtual cards, and mandatory multi-signature human approval for transactions exceeding $10.

5
00:00:45,460 --> 00:00:58,660
We implement a strict circuit breaker pattern in Python: if an agent spends more than $5 in any 10-minute sliding window, all outbound API keys are instantly frozen until a verified human administrator clicks approve in a Telegram webhook.

6
00:00:59,560 --> 00:01:08,390
This completely eliminates both accidental runaway recursive loops and deliberate financial denial-of-wallet attacks initiated by malicious actors.

7
00:01:09,290 --> 00:01:13,597
Financial boundaries preserve organizational stability.

8
00:01:13,597 --> 00:01:21,430
Let us examine the AP2 payment protocol on Slide 29 to see how we enforce these cryptographic rules.

